## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cost-aware routing and per-tenant quotas, a practical constraint in real llm-ops gateways.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant budgets and token accounting
- Routing policy using model variants (fast/balanced/cheap)
- Fallback mechanisms and caching
- API surface and minimal test plan

## Code Example

```javascript
function route(request, budget, models){
  const t = request.tokens;
  if (budget.remaining < t) return {model: models.cheap, useCache:true};
  // choose model based on remaining budget
  // ...
  return {model: models.balanced, remaining: budget.remaining - t};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test budget rollover at day boundary?
- How handle sudden budget spike without harming SLA?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes understanding of per-tenant isolation, backpressure, and queueing strategies under load, a common real-world challenge in llm-ops gateways.

## Key Concepts
- Priority queues and scheduling (WFQ, weighted RR)
- Backpressure and saturation handling
- Tenant isolation and SLA guarantees
- Observability: latency, tail latency, fairness

## Code Example
```javascript
class Scheduler {
  constructor() {
    this.queues = {Gold: [], Silver: [], Bronze: []};
  }
  enqueue(tenant, priority, job) {
    this.queues[priority].push({tenant, job});
  }
  dequeue() {
    for (const p of ['Gold','Silver','Bronze']) {
      if (this.queues[p].length) return this.queues[p].shift();
    }
    return null;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure and ensure Gold latency bounds under burst?
- How would you adapt weights if Bronze saturates frequently?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical skills in enforcing privacy, security, and auditability in a multi-tenant LLM-ops gateway. It requires concrete architecture and measurable guarantees beyond high-level design.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy enforcement
- Deterministic validation and replayability
- PII detection and redaction
- Prompt-injection guardrails
- Latency budgeting and tamper-evident logging

## Code Example

```javascript
function validatePrompt(prompt, policy) {
  // normalize
  let p = prompt.trim();
  // redaction rules (pseudo)
  policy.redactionPatterns.forEach(r => p = p.replace(r, 'REDACTED'));
  // injection guard (very simple placeholder)
  const blocked = policy.injectionPatterns.some(pat => new RegExp(pat, 'i').test(p));
  return { valid: !blocked, redacted: p };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy coverage across tenants with different privacy rules?
- How would you handle policy updates without affecting in-flight prompts?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to design governance-aware, low-latency LLM gating in multi-tenant contexts with compliance, verifiable provenance, and drift detection.

## Key Concepts
- Policy schema design for strict data governance
- Real-time evaluation and routing with canary semantics
- Tamper-evident logging and prompt hashing
- Drift and latency testing in distributed infra

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple policy eval skeleton
function evaluatePolicy(prompt, policy) {
  const redacted = applyRedaction(prompt, policy.redaction_rules);
  const hash = hashPrompt(redacted, policy);
  const allowed = policy.allowed;
  return { allowed, redacted, hash };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift across regions?
- How would you ensure logs remain tamper-evident during partial outages?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable, privacy-aware routing under mixed latency budgets and per-tenant policies. Requires integration of policy engines, redaction, quotas, data locality, auditability, and testing under burst traffic.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy modeling
- Latency budgeting and SLA targets
- Model variant routing and cost-speed tradeoffs
- Redaction at prompt and response with data locality
- Tamper-evident auditing and drift detection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo routing decision example
function decideRoute(ctx) {
  const tenant = ctx.tenant
  const policy = policyStore[tenant] || policyStore['default']
  // Evaluate latency and redact flags
  const model = policy.chooseModel(ctx.prompt)
  const redact = policy.shouldRedact(ctx.prompt)
  return { model, redact }
}
```

